WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.240
Hello, I am Kovid Goyal, the creator of Kitty. In this video, I will give you a brief tour

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:13.640
of Kitty's productivity enhancing features. Kitty started as a personal project to enhance

3
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:18.520
my own terminal experience, but has since grown into something used by thousands of people

4
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:25.640
the world over. Kitty is designed for terminal power users, people who, like me, prefer to

5
00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:31.520
spend the entire day in the terminal and use the terminal for every task imaginable. Kitty has

6
00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.800
many features and niceties that make this kind of workflow both more productive and more fun.

7
00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:45.440
Let's start with something simple. Often, when running commands in the terminal, it so happens

8
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:51.960
that a command produces lots of output and takes a long time to run. You might find yourself

9
00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:58.040
wanting to examine that output in more detail. You can, of course, scroll up and just look at the

10
00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:03.880
output, but sometimes it's useful to open it in an editor or a pager and say search through it

11
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:10.360
or filter it. Kitty has a built-in feature wherein if you press the Control Shift and G key,

12
00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:16.120
the output from the last command opens in the pager. As you can see here, the output from the

13
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:23.480
ls command is opened in a pager: less, in my case. This is not limited to just the last command. You

14
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:29.800
can actually scroll up and here you can see a compile output from building Kitty to examine the

15
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.880
output of any previous command. You hold the Control Shift key and right click on the output

16
00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:41.400
and there you go. It opens in the pager as before. I can now search through it or refer to it

17
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:48.760
to my heart's content. You can even use Kitty's multiple windows feature to have the pager window

18
00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:56.520
pop up as a separate window. You can see here there are now two windows. One has the original shell

19
00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:05.640
and one has the output from the clicked command open in a pager. I can scroll through this

20
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:10.760
output and refer to it while typing future commands. I find this facility extremely handy and

21
00:02:10.760 --> 00:02:17.160
use it several times every day. Note that this feature is implemented in Kitty by means of

22
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.680
shell integration. Shell integration means that Kitty works with common shells such as bash,

23
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:29.080
Z shell, fish etc. to enable this and other niceties. For example, you will notice that the current

24
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:34.600
working directory is shown in the window title bar. If I change to some other directory,

25
00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:40.760
the window title bar changes. Similarly, if I run a command, say for example, sleep,

26
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.160
the name of the command shows up in the window title bar while it's running. Once the sleep is over,

27
00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:53.800
the title bar goes back to the working directory. This is a small example of some of Kitty's

28
00:02:53.800 --> 00:03:01.160
productivity features that make working the terminal all day much more fun and productive.

29
00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:08.840
Now, let's try something a little more fancy. Consider that most quotidian of terminal

30
00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.800
experiences listing files. So here we go. Let's list some files.

31
00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:21.080
Looks pretty standard so far. A rather colorful list of files and a couple of directories.

32
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:27.720
The fun starts when you click on one of these files. So for example, let's see how Kitty is

33
00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:33.480
launched. Holding down the control shift keys, I click on it. And as you can see,

34
00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:40.280
the launcher.c file has opened up in my editor of choice, which is vim. And I can edit it to do

35
00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:46.520
whatever. Simply by clicking on the file. And that's not all. You can even click on an image.

36
00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:56.200
For example, let's click on the Kitty logo. And there we go. It opens in the terminal. You can view

37
00:03:56.200 --> 00:04:03.160
the image. So as you can see, simply clicking on files on the listing, you can open them.

38
00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:09.240
And of course, Kitty is meant for power users, which means that you don't have to use the mouse.

39
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:18.200
You can instead press control shift P and Y. And it lists all the files with a number

40
00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:23.960
next to their names. So for example, if I press six, once again, the Kitty logo opens up.

41
00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:34.600
These features are powered by two things. The first is the ls command has a

42
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:39.400
facility to output hyperlinks. So let's see

43
00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:50.440
here the ls command on my computer. As you can see here, as the hyperlink auto flag

44
00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:58.600
added to it, this means that every file that ls lists is actually a hyperlink. You can see

45
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:04.280
that it gets underlined when you move the mouse over it. The second thing that makes this possible

46
00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:14.040
is a piece of configuration in Kitty called open-actions.conf. In this file, you can tell Kitty

47
00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:21.800
what to do when you open a file, for example, by clicking on it. So here, you can see that this

48
00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:30.840
entry on this line tells Kitty that for files of my type text, open it in the editor in an overlay

49
00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:37.240
window, which means a window overlayed on the current window. Similarly, image files are opened

50
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:48.280
in Kitty itself using the icat kitten and so on. This entry here is of particular interest. It uses

51
00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:56.600
another kitten, which is a small program that's part of Kitty, to allow you to directly click on

52
00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:03.720
search results from a grep tool and open them in an editor. So let's see that in action.

53
00:06:04.680 --> 00:06:13.240
Here we go. Let me run my grep tool, which, and search for say, hyperlink.

54
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:21.320
Here you can see a bunch of results. So moving the mouse over these results, they get underlined,

55
00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:27.800
indicating they're clickable. So let's open this one and there you see it has opened in the editor

56
00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:34.600
right on the click line. This is extremely convenient to inserting through files in the terminal.

57
00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:49.160
This in turn is powered by, let's see here what we have and the kitten. Now kittens are small

58
00:06:49.160 --> 00:06:53.880
kitty programs that enhance its functionality. So here the kitten is the hyperlinked grep kitten.

59
00:06:54.760 --> 00:07:00.040
So what that does is it takes the output from a grep program and adds hyperlinks to it so that

60
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:07.560
they become clickable when displayed in Kitty. So as you can see here, these simple features that

61
00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:14.280
are designed in a composable and modular way allow you to greatly enhance the experience of

62
00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:23.080
using a terminal. Since we have been talking of hyperlinks, let's note that the usefulness

63
00:07:23.080 --> 00:07:29.000
is not limited only to the local machine. You can even use them over SSH connections to remote

64
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:39.880
servers. So for example, let me SSH into one of my servers and do an ls hyperlink auto.

65
00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:48.200
You can see some files have been listed. One of them is an image. Let's click on this image and

66
00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:54.440
see what happens. As you can see it opens up in a kitty kitten. This is the remote file kitten

67
00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:59.400
and it gives you some options for what you want to do with the image. You can edit it. You can

68
00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:04.520
open it. You can save it to your local computer. So let's choose to open it to view the file locally.

69
00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:16.200
There we go. The remote file kitten seamlessly downloads the image in the background and opens it

70
00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:24.920
locally on the local computer. This is useful if particularly for viewing a complicated file such as

71
00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:33.160
images, videos, etc. that the remote server may not be able to handle. But the remote file kitten

72
00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:38.600
is not limited simply to opening. You can also edit files. So for example, here we have a simple

73
00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:48.360
text file. Let's click on it and once again the remote file kitten opens.

74
00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.960
It gives you the option to edit the file. So let's choose that option by pressing the E key.

75
00:08:56.360 --> 00:09:02.600
You can see that the kitten downloads the file and let's make some changes to it.

76
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:10.200
Notice that this is running in vim on my local machine with all the customization and

77
00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:19.400
facilities available. Let me save and quit vim. The remote file kitten automatically

78
00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:26.360
updates the file on the server. Let's cat it again. And you see that the file has been edited

79
00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:32.600
as expected. This is really useful if you tend to spend a lot of time SSH

80
00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:38.840
into servers and you need to edit, make a one off edit using a fancy editor or similar.

81
00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:47.000
I should note that as far as it comes to images, you can actually view images directly over

82
00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:53.960
SSH connections thanks to yet another kitten, the icat image viewing kitten in kitty. We'll discuss

83
00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:58.840
that and some more fancy kittens in the following segment.

84
00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:06.200
Now it's time to have some fun with images. I know terminals are text devices but sometimes

85
00:10:06.200 --> 00:10:10.120
it's cool to be able to display images and even useful if you're SSH into a remote computer.

86
00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:14.680
I need to quickly look at a graph or a report that's available as an image.

87
00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:22.520
So without further ado, let's see how we do that. We use the icat kitten which is a

88
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:29.000
small program written that's distributed as part of kitty that can display images within kitty.

89
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.440
Here I'll display the kitty logo as you can see shows up as an image in the right there in the

90
00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:41.400
terminal itself. The icat kitten can also download images from the internet and display them.

91
00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:47.640
Here we'll see it doing that with an animated gif of a Rubik's cube. I have icat

92
00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:50.680
aliased as just the icat command on my computer.

93
00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:57.880
So here you see it downloaded the Rubik's cube and it's displaying the animation. The animation

94
00:10:57.880 --> 00:11:05.640
will continue playing even while you type other commands. The kitty graphics protocol that powers

95
00:11:05.640 --> 00:11:10.920
the icat kitten is integrated with many other programs. For example, the Ranger file manager.

96
00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.800
Here you see we've opened a folder full of images. And as you scroll through the files,

97
00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:23.400
Ranger displays thumbnails of the images using the kitty graphics protocol right there in the

98
00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:30.200
terminal. Another cool feature of the kitty graphics protocol is that it works over SSH. So if I

99
00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:45.000
SSH into one of my servers and run icat Athenaum.jpg, you see that the image is displayed directly

100
00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:51.880
in the terminal even though it's on a remote server. The image data is sent over the TTY pipe

101
00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:58.120
over SSH. When running locally, the graphics protocol is smart enough to avoid the overhead of

102
00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:06.280
doing this and to use the local file system to display images. Another cool kitten that kitty has

103
00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:12.600
is the unicode input kitten, which you can use to input arbitrary unicode symbols, emoji, etc.

104
00:12:12.600 --> 00:12:18.440
into your terminal. Just press control shift U and then type the name of the symbol you want. So

105
00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:25.720
for example, here we have a cat. We can insert a cat face with tears of joy into our terminal

106
00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:33.240
and there you have it right there at the prompt. You can search for all sorts of symbols. Here we have

107
00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:43.800
some beer. Let's celebrate this great kitten right here. So this is useful. If you find yourself

108
00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:49.160
doing a lot of chatting using terminal chat programs such as WeChat and you want to send your

109
00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:57.240
buddies emoji or other other symbols. We have talked a fair bit about kittens and how they can

110
00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:02.520
be used to enhance the terminal experience. Now let's discuss something more basic window management.

111
00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:08.360
Kitty has the ability to tile multiple windows next to each other and manage them using flexible

112
00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:14.120
layouts. You can also create multiple tabs. So let's create some windows by pressing control shift

113
00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:19.880
enter. You see we have three windows tiles next to each other. We can create some new tabs as well.

114
00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:25.080
The tabs are displayed at the bottom. The tab titles show the current working directory

115
00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:32.840
or actually whatever title the program running in the shell sets. So let's close these tabs.

116
00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:39.480
Get back to the windows. As you can see these windows are tiled next to each other. You can press

117
00:13:39.480 --> 00:13:45.800
control shift and the window number to switch to a window. So one for the first two for the second

118
00:13:45.800 --> 00:13:52.520
three for the third and so on. You can even visually shift by pressing control shift F7 and simply

119
00:13:52.520 --> 00:14:00.040
typing the number of the window you want to shift to. Now while we are discussing multiple windows

120
00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:05.160
let's look at a cool feature whereby we can type in one window and have the text appear in all

121
00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:11.800
the windows simultaneously. This is useful if you want to do a poor man's automation of multiple

122
00:14:11.800 --> 00:14:18.280
servers for example. To do this let's open the kitty shell which we can do by pressing control shift

123
00:14:18.280 --> 00:14:23.800
escape. The kitty shell is a shell where you can issue commands to kitty itself to make it do things.

124
00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:31.560
So here let's first change the layout to grid which works better with four windows

125
00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:42.520
and now to actually broadcast the text we need to launch the broadcast kitten which we can do

126
00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:56.920
like this. And we see that we have a broadcast window available at which we can type. Let me type

127
00:14:57.000 --> 00:15:03.080
the command ls. You see that ls has been typed in every single window. You can backspace and it works

128
00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:09.080
as expected. Let's execute the ls command and we see that the ls has been executed in every window

129
00:15:09.080 --> 00:15:16.360
showing different outputs. Let's clear out these outputs and end the broadcast session.

130
00:15:18.280 --> 00:15:23.560
So as you can see here the kitty shell is quite a powerful tool.

131
00:15:24.440 --> 00:15:31.000
A note that the shell is powered by something called the kitty remote control protocol.

132
00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:36.840
This allows you to control kitty from other shells, other terminals, programs running on your

133
00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:42.120
computer etc. And this is very powerful. Anything you can do in the shell you can also do

134
00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:48.360
via the remote control facility. Let's have a quick look at it. Change the layout to stack so we

135
00:15:48.920 --> 00:15:54.360
focused on the shell window. Let's see some commands. So here you can see a list of commands

136
00:15:54.360 --> 00:15:59.400
available in the shell. You can do things like create new windows, set window titles, set window

137
00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:08.760
logos, send text to windows, set change the opacity, select windows, all sorts of things resize them,

138
00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:15.240
send signals to children running in windows etc. You can change the colors, you can run kittens,

139
00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:23.560
and on and on and on. So this forms a general scripting ability for kitty and allows you to

140
00:16:23.560 --> 00:16:30.840
control kitty and automate various tasks in it. Your imagination is the limit when it comes to

141
00:16:30.840 --> 00:16:38.920
use cases. Since this is supposed to be a brief tour I think I'll end here but before I go

142
00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:44.680
let's see how to bling-up the kitty terminal a bit by changing its colors. Kitty actually has

143
00:16:44.680 --> 00:16:52.440
a themes kitten that has a large collection of themes downloaded from a central repository.

144
00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:57.720
Let's run it and see what happens. So here you can see there are literally hundreds of themes

145
00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:06.680
organized by dark and light colors. You can choose a theme you want. So for example I like

146
00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:15.640
Belafonte. So let's change to that by pressing the Enter key and then the M key to tell the kitten

147
00:17:15.640 --> 00:17:21.880
to modify kitty.com to make the change permanent and you see that kitty now uses the Belafonte theme.

148
00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:28.120
Note that the kitten can actually be run non interactively as well. So for example if you specify

149
00:17:28.120 --> 00:17:35.000
the theme name on the command line it will change the theme directly without running interactively.

150
00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:41.960
You can actually choose where all to change the colors either in the current terminal or

151
00:17:41.960 --> 00:17:47.880
all running terminals on your system. So as you can see the themes kitten is a nice way to

152
00:17:49.240 --> 00:17:55.080
make your terminal experience a little more colorful. I personally use it to adjust the colors

153
00:17:55.080 --> 00:18:03.720
to the time of day so that the screen ambient brightness is the best match for my eyes.

154
00:18:03.720 --> 00:18:12.440
And I think with that I'll say goodbye and thank you and hope you enjoy using kitty.
